# Conclusion

This project developed a machine-learning pipeline for detecting fraudulent financial
transactions in a highly imbalanced dataset.

The dataset contains approximately 284,807 transactions, of which only 492 are fraudulent,
meaning that fraud represents approximately 0.17% of all transactions.

Because of this severe class imbalance, accuracy was not used as the primary evaluation
metric. Instead, Precision, Recall, F1-score, and ROC-AUC were used.

SMOTE was applied to the training data to address class imbalance while preserving the original
test distribution.

Two machine-learning models were evaluated:

1. Logistic Regression
2. Random Forest

The models were compared using fraud-focused performance metrics.

Recall is particularly important because false negatives represent fraudulent transactions
that the system fails to detect. However, precision must also be considered because excessive
false positives can create unnecessary investigations and inconvenience legitimate customers.

Feature importance and Logistic Regression coefficients were examined to understand which
features contributed most strongly to predictions.

For production deployment at approximately one million transactions per hour, the model
would need a scalable inference architecture involving streaming or batch processing,
efficient feature engineering, model serving, monitoring, and periodic retraining.

Overall, fraud detection requires balancing statistical performance with business costs,
especially the trade-off between detecting more fraudulent transactions and generating
manageable numbers of false alerts.
